/*
We have two lanes, each of length 1 (only relative times matter).
Swimmer i needs p[i] time to swim one length.

All swimmers start together from one end.
They can only overtake at the ends, so within a lane segment they move in the
order they started that segment; faster swimmers behind slower ones are blocked
and arrive together with the slowest swimmer in front of them.

At each end, swimmers waiting there may reorder arbitrarily by natural pace
before starting the next length.

Thus, for each length, if a set of swimmers starts in one lane in some order,
the arrival times are prefix maxima of their p's in that order.
To minimize total completion time, at every departure from an end we should send
swimmers in nondecreasing p order in each lane, and split them between the two
lanes. For a sorted sequence assigned to one lane, completion times for that
length are just the p's themselves.

This means each length behaves independently as processing jobs of durations p[i]
on 2 identical machines, where a swimmer needing k lengths contributes k jobs,
one per remaining length, and all jobs are available at the corresponding end
when the swimmer arrives there.

A simpler equivalent view:
Whenever swimmers are at an end, the time until all of them reach the other end
is the optimal 2-machine makespan for their one-length times p[i], i.e. partition
into two groups minimizing the maximum group sum.

After crossing, swimmers with remaining laps continue from the new end; swimmers
finished leave. Repeat.

Since each swimmer alternates ends, swimmers present at an end at any stage are
exactly those with remaining lengths and correct parity. We can simulate stage by
stage. The number of stages is max(laps[i]), where laps means number of lengths.

For each stage, among swimmers still active, all are at the current end together.
The minimal time for this stage is partition DP on their p[i].

Constraints are not given in the prompt, so we use bitset subset-sum per stage,
which is standard and efficient when total pace sum is moderate. If needed, this
can be adapted, but this is the natural exact solution.
*/

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n;
    if (!(cin >> n)) return 0;

    vector<int> p(n), l(n);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) cin >> p[i] >> l[i];

    int maxL = 0;
    for (int x : l) maxL = max(maxL, x);

    long long answer = 0;

    for (int stage = 1; stage <= maxL; ++stage) {
        vector<int> cur;
        int sum = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
            if (l[i] >= stage) {
                cur.push_back(p[i]);
                sum += p[i];
            }
        }
        if (cur.empty()) continue;

        vector<char> dp(sum + 1, 0);
        dp[0] = 1;
        int reach = 0;
        for (int x : cur) {
            for (int s = reach; s >= 0; --s) {
                if (dp[s]) dp[s + x] = 1;
            }
            reach += x;
        }

        int best = sum;
        for (int s = 0; s <= sum; ++s) if (dp[s]) {
            best = min(best, max(s, sum - s));
        }
        answer += best;
    }

    cout << answer << '\n';
    return 0;
}

